pkg:Evidence_v1_8_0 a quality:Evidence, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Gate transcript for code-abundance-rdodi v1.8.0 (MINOR release)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "MINOR: user challenged whether a single Epic could genuinely match the richness of all 4 RDODI stages' own gate requirements, and asked for real domain engineering rather than defended structure. Re-read the RDODI procedure's actual gate tables for all 4 stages and the 5 cross-stage gates directly, then checked concrete current state rather than assumed. Two real, previously-unowned findings: (1) Cross.D (all 4 stage files at one coherent release version) genuinely fails right now -- checked directly: v1.0.0/v1.1.0/v1.2.0/v1.1.0, four different versions, no backlog item addressed this. Added STORY-5. (2) Cross.A (Stage2=Stage3=Stage4 section count, 1:1:1) genuinely fails (25=25, page=21) -- but the deeper finding, confirmed by reading the corpus-instantiation template's own schema directly, is that STORY-4 as originally scoped ('sync 21 sections to 25') was the wrong fix for the artifact this Epic is building: the template organizes content as CATEGORIES(7) x PRINCIPLES(18) = 25, matching Stage 2's taxonomy exactly, but as a two-level hierarchy, not 25 flat page sections. Cross.A's literal wording does not transfer to that structure; its actual intent (full traceable coverage) does. Rescoped STORY-4 accordingly, resolving the obsolescence question left open for 3 turns. Checked before adding: whether ExecutionTask should exist yet -- its own definition names it as produced by planning a PBI into an iteration; with zero PlanningEvent/Iteration in this backlog, creating one now would repeat the checkpoint-fabrication error already caught once. Correctly stays absent. Backlog now 1 Epic, 5 Stories (was 4). Re-verified: pipeline order (Stage_Backlog digest recomputed to 30 subjects, reproduces; earlier stages unaffected), full SHACL 0 violations."@en ;
    pub:transcriptSHA256 "f9a1d4ef0c6428acc1fa67e851dd5bce6facc95be7ce43a0a43be21f6c2bd551"^^xsd:string .
